  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 134 AM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nMonday will be the first day of areawide seasonably cool weather. It \nwill feel bitter compared to yesterday's highs (20 to 25 degrees \nabove normal), but in reality we'll still be within the range of \nnormal with highs in the 40s. Tuesday, the day the cold airmass gets \nclosest to us, will be the coolest day of the week with highs \nranging from the upper 30s to mid-40s from northwest to southeast. \nThankfully, the proximity of the surface high will keep winds \ndampened, so wind chill values will only be a few degrees cooler \nthan the ambient air temperature. Warm air advection will pick back \nup on Wednesday as the low-level ridge scoots east and winds turn \nout of the southeast. For the rest of the week, including \nThanksgiving, temperatures will gradually moderate.\n\nThe next chance for precipitation will come Wednesday into \nThanksgiving Day, but confidence in the forecast during this \ntimeframe is still very low. Starting off: Is it possible someone \nsees a snowflake late this week? Yes, it is possible. Is it possible \nsomeone sees impactful snow accumulations? Almost certainly not.\n\nThe forecast as it stands right now shows an upper-level northern \nstream closed-low diving through the central CONUS and phasing with \nanother closed-low or deep trough. As these features potentially \nFujiwhara somewhere in the Midwest, a low-level inverted trough is \nforecast to develop in the Southeast with moisture shunting \nnorthward along its axis as it amplifies into the Ohio Valley. This \npush of moisture brings with it the chance of precipitation mainly \nin our Illinois counties as it passes sometime Wednesday into early \nThanksgiving morning. \n\nDeterministic guidance still varies immensely regarding the timing, \namplitude, phasing, etc. of the upper level features, and in turn, \nwhat will happen at the surface. The ECMWF and Canadian have been \ntrending more westerly with their precipitation chances (toward our \nCWA), but even then we're still only grazed by the system. If this \ntrend holds true and we do anticipate precipitation, the potential \nfor snow is low and the potential for snow-driven impacts is very \nlow for a number of reasons. First, antecedent warmth. Though lows \nthis week will fall below freezing each morning, highs will \nconsistently remain well above freezing. This will not provide a \ncold enough primer for snow to stick. Second, sounding data only \nsupports snow for a few hours max. Even then it's usually preceded \nby rain, and there is subsidence in the low-levels and in the \nunsaturated snow growth zone. Third, some model guidance is pushing \nthe precip through the area during the day. If that is what happens, \ntemperatures will be well above freezing, making snow an \nimpossibility. None of these factors support impactful snow. Again, \nit's possible that light snow does fall Wednesday into Thanksgiving, \nbut the other puzzle pieces create a very benign picture surrounding \nit.",
